1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,248
<font color="#000000">This paper introduces DGAPN, the Distilled Graph
Attention Policy Network,</font>

2
00:00:05,328 --> 00:00:11,641
<font color="#000000">a reinforcement learning framework for generating
drug-like molecules that bind antiviral targets.</font>

3
00:00:11,721 --> 00:00:16,338
<font color="#000000">It combines spatial graph attention encoding
chemistry and 3D structure,</font>

4
00:00:16,418 --> 00:00:18,686
<font color="#000000">a fragment-based attentional policy,</font>

5
00:00:18,766 --> 00:00:22,274
<font color="#000000">and a curiosity bonus from random network distillation,</font>

6
00:00:22,354 --> 00:00:25,210
<font color="#000000">beating prior generators on binding affinity.</font>

7
00:00:25,590 --> 00:00:28,489
<font color="#000000">Designing molecules that optimize a property,</font>

8
00:00:28,569 --> 00:00:33,586
<font color="#000000">like binding tightly to a viral protein, is a
central goal in drug discovery,</font>

9
00:00:33,666 --> 00:00:34,976
<font color="#000000">yet hard to automate.</font>

10
00:00:35,056 --> 00:00:38,683
<font color="#000000">The chemical space is discrete and astronomically large,</font>

11
00:00:38,763 --> 00:00:40,602
<font color="#000000">making exploration difficult,</font>

12
00:00:40,682 --> 00:00:44,905
<font color="#000000">and compressing a molecule's atom-and-bond graph
into a faithful,</font>

13
00:00:44,985 --> 00:00:47,950
<font color="#000000">learnable representation is itself nontrivial.</font>

14
00:00:48,330 --> 00:00:51,352
<font color="#000000">Prior methods leave value on the table twice.</font>

15
00:00:51,432 --> 00:00:56,109
<font color="#000000">First, most graph networks encode only atom
attributes and adjacency,</font>

16
00:00:56,189 --> 00:00:58,797
<font color="#000000">ignoring bond features and 3D geometry,</font>

17
00:00:58,877 --> 00:01:04,312
<font color="#000000">even though shape and complementarity to the
receptor pocket make a good binder.</font>

18
00:01:04,392 --> 00:01:07,828
<font color="#000000">Second, building molecules atom by atom gives long,</font>

19
00:01:07,908 --> 00:01:11,551
<font color="#000000">unstable trajectories and hard-to-synthesize products.</font>

20
00:01:11,631 --> 00:01:16,790
<font color="#000000">This motivates encoding spatial structure while
acting over whole fragments.</font>

21
00:01:17,170 --> 00:01:20,756
<font color="#000000">DGAPN makes three contributions.</font>

22
00:01:20,836 --> 00:01:24,423
<font color="#000000">First, spatial Graph Attention,</font>

23
00:01:24,503 --> 00:01:28,089
<font color="#000000">a GNN that self-attends over node and edge
attributes and encodes 3D structure via an inverse distance matrix.</font>

24
00:01:28,169 --> 00:01:31,755
<font color="#000000">Second, the Graph Attention Policy Network,</font>

25
00:01:31,835 --> 00:01:35,421
<font color="#000000">an attentional policy over a dynamic fragment
action space trained with PPO.</font>

26
00:01:35,501 --> 00:01:39,088
<font color="#000000">Third,</font>

27
00:01:39,168 --> 00:01:42,754
<font color="#000000">a random-network-distillation curiosity bonus
giving innovation rewards for high-affinity,</font>

28
00:01:42,834 --> 00:01:46,420
<font color="#000000">synthesizable molecules.</font>

29
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:50,214
<font color="#000000">DGAPN casts molecule generation as a Markov
decision process.</font>

30
00:01:50,294 --> 00:01:53,708
<font color="#000000">At each step,</font>

31
00:01:53,788 --> 00:01:57,203
<font color="#000000">the CReM library proposes valid molecules reachable
by swapping one fragment,</font>

32
00:01:57,283 --> 00:02:00,697
<font color="#000000">guaranteeing synthesizable candidates.</font>

33
00:02:00,777 --> 00:02:04,191
<font color="#000000">Spatial Graph Attention embeds each candidate,</font>

34
00:02:04,271 --> 00:02:07,685
<font color="#000000">combining attention over atom and bond attributes
with a spatial convolution from a sparsified</font>

35
00:02:07,765 --> 00:02:11,179
<font color="#000000">inverse distance matrix,</font>

36
00:02:11,259 --> 00:02:14,673
<font color="#000000">so chemistry and geometry both inform the representation.</font>

37
00:02:14,753 --> 00:02:18,168
<font color="#000000">An attentional policy scores candidates and samples
the next molecule,</font>

38
00:02:18,248 --> 00:02:21,662
<font color="#000000">trained with PPO.</font>

39
00:02:21,742 --> 00:02:25,156
<font color="#000000">Finally,</font>

40
00:02:25,236 --> 00:02:28,650
<font color="#000000">random network distillation supplies an innovation
reward whose error rewards exploring novel states.</font>

41
00:02:29,030 --> 00:02:35,350
<font color="#000000">The primary benchmark designs novel inhibitors
binding the NSP15 site of SARS-CoV-2.</font>

42
00:02:35,430 --> 00:02:39,236
<font color="#000000">Binding affinity is estimated by molecular docking,</font>

43
00:02:39,316 --> 00:02:43,731
<font color="#000000">using a GPU-accelerated tool on the protein's 3D
structure,</font>

44
00:02:43,811 --> 00:02:49,445
<font color="#000000">starting from a dataset of purchasable molecules
with NSP15 docking scores.</font>

45
00:02:49,525 --> 00:02:55,540
<font color="#000000">The method is also tested on standard QED and
penalized LogP optimization tasks.</font>

46
00:02:55,920 --> 00:03:01,032
<font color="#000000">On docking against NSP15, DGAPN clearly beats five
state-of-the-art baselines,</font>

47
00:03:01,112 --> 00:03:06,291
<font color="#000000">reaching a best score near minus ten and a mean of
minus six point seven seven,</font>

48
00:03:06,371 --> 00:03:11,617
<font color="#000000">well ahead of MolDQN, with an astronomically small
p-value under Welch's t-test.</font>

49
00:03:11,697 --> 00:03:16,144
<font color="#000000">It also wins constrained optimization at every
similarity threshold,</font>

50
00:03:16,224 --> 00:03:23,200
<font color="#000000">and in multi-objective mode trades slight affinity
for markedly better drug-likeness and synthesizability.</font>

51
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:27,259
<font color="#000000">The authors ablate each component.</font>

52
00:03:27,339 --> 00:03:31,018
<font color="#000000">In a supervised setting on NSP15,</font>

53
00:03:31,097 --> 00:03:34,776
<font color="#000000">loss curves over forty runs show spatial
convolution strongly improves molecular representation learning.</font>

54
00:03:34,856 --> 00:03:38,535
<font color="#000000">Comparing full DGAPN against GAPN without the
innovation reward,</font>

55
00:03:38,615 --> 00:03:42,294
<font color="#000000">the curiosity bonus lifts the best docking score,</font>

56
00:03:42,374 --> 00:03:46,052
<font color="#000000">though it slightly worsens synthetic accessibility.</font>

57
00:03:46,132 --> 00:03:49,811
<font color="#000000">Remarkably,</font>

58
00:03:49,891 --> 00:03:53,570
<font color="#000000">DGAPN even beats a greedy CReM oracle that sees
intermediate docking rewards.</font>

59
00:03:53,950 --> 00:03:58,233
<font color="#000000">On NSP15 docking,</font>

60
00:03:58,313 --> 00:04:02,597
<font color="#000000">DGAPN reaches a best score near minus ten and a
mean of minus six point seven seven,</font>

61
00:04:02,677 --> 00:04:06,960
<font color="#000000">versus roughly minus eight and minus five for the
strongest baseline.</font>

62
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:11,323
<font color="#000000">The gap over the second-best model is statistically
overwhelming,</font>

63
00:04:11,403 --> 00:04:15,687
<font color="#000000">p around ten to the minus two hundred ninth.</font>

64
00:04:15,767 --> 00:04:20,050
<font color="#000000">In multi-objective mode it yields QED zero point
seven two and synthetic accessibility two point two.</font>

65
00:04:20,430 --> 00:04:25,964
<font color="#000000">The takeaway: combining spatial structure with
curiosity pays off for molecular design.</font>

66
00:04:26,044 --> 00:04:29,578
<font color="#000000">By encoding 3D geometry through spatial graph attention,</font>

67
00:04:29,658 --> 00:04:32,741
<font color="#000000">acting over whole fragments for synthesizability,</font>

68
00:04:32,821 --> 00:04:36,290
<font color="#000000">and rewarding novelty with random network distillation,</font>

69
00:04:36,370 --> 00:04:41,775
<font color="#000000">DGAPN designs tighter-binding, more synthesizable
drug candidates than prior methods,</font>

70
00:04:41,855 --> 00:04:46,680
<font color="#000000">with a user-defined objective that dials between
affinity and drug-likeness.</font>
